{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>If the anointed priest shall sin.</b> The order of the sinners who bring an offering in this perashah is: the High Priest, and the Sanhedrin, and the king of Israel, and afterward the multitudes of the people. And He wished to begin with the priest, for he is the great man and the angel of the Hosts of YHWH, and from him the rest of Israel would see and thus act — that they would take care to return in repentance. For when they see that the most distinguished among them, the priest who is their head, brings an offering for his sin, all will see and take admonition and learn from him a fortiori: if the Holy One, blessed be He, grants atonement to one who is near to Him and who was not worthy of sinning, how much more so will He atone for the rest of the people. For it is well known that according to a person's eminence, his transgression is weightier. And of this Solomon said, peace be upon him (Ecclesiastes 1:18): ״כי ברוב חכמה רב כעס״ — for in much wisdom is much anger — according as one is wiser and more righteous, the anger of his sin and his error before the Holy One, blessed be He, is weightier, and the proof is from Moses our teacher, peace be upon him." "<b>To the guilt of the people.</b> According to the habit and way of the people, for they are guilty; therefore He added 'to the guilt of the people,' in order to admonish concerning repentance and that the sinner not be ashamed to return from his sin. And thus Scripture explained: ״על אחת מכל אשר יעשה האדם לחטוא בהנה״ — for any one of all the things that a person does to sin thereby — that is, from what he is accustomed to do and is prone to regarding sin, meaning to say that he should bring his offering and it will be atoned for him, for he is one accustomed to sin and his way is thus, and likewise the way of the people in guilt and iniquity — the way of the Holy One, blessed be He, is to atone his sin for him, for such is His attribute." "<b>And from this</b> they said in the Midrash on Lamentations (Lamentations 3:42): ״נחנו פשענו ומרינו״ — we have transgressed and rebelled — ״כמדתנו אתה לא סלחת, כמדתך״ — according to our measure You did not forgive; according to Your measure. For the attribute of the Holy One, blessed be He, is that even for the complete wicked person who multiplies sin, He multiplies forgiveness if he returns. And this is what the prophet admonishes (Isaiah 55:7): ״יעזוב רשע דרכו ואיש און מחשבותיו״ — let the wicked one forsake his way and the man of iniquity his thoughts — and the term 'wicked one' refers to one who is arrogant and denies the root principle, thinking that there is no Elohim in the heavens and no providence on earth, in the manner of what is written (Psalms 10:4): ״רשע כגובה אפו בל ידרוש אין אלהים כל מזמותיו״ — the wicked in the pride of his countenance does not seek; there is no Elohim, all his schemes. And even so, when he returns in repentance the Holy One, blessed be He, receives him, and needless to say he will find forgiveness in the attribute of mercy, for even in the attribute of justice he will find forgiveness; and this is what it says (Isaiah 55:7): ״וישוב אל ה׳ וירחמהו ואל אלהינו כי ירבה לסלוח״ — and let him return to YHWH and He will have compassion on him, and to our Elohim, for He multiplies forgiveness — that is, even in the attribute of justice. And likewise we find in the psalm ״נאום פשע לרשע״ (Psalms 36:2), where it is written there (Psalms 36:6): ״ה׳ בהשמים חסדך״ — O YHWH, Your kindness is in the heavens — and that psalm is founded on the deeds of the wicked who deny, and therefore it mentions there the attribute of kindness, because the kindness of the Holy One, blessed be He, is proclaimed among the nations through His forgiveness of the wicked. And because he mentioned that the kindness is in the heavens, therefore he added the letter ה, for the ה is the shape of an opening (פתח), and it alludes to this world (עוה״ז); and in order to teach that even on earth, in this world which was created with the ה, an open doorway to repentance may be found — even the absolute wicked person, the Holy One, blessed be He, will draw His kindness to him if he wishes to return."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:22:51.017235485Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}